title: "Ghost Infrastructure? Testing Whether 19th-Century Coal-Mining Geography Shapes 15-Minute Walking Access in Bochum and Essen, Germany"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0002-5683-5966"
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/GHOST_INFRASTRUCTURE"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/GHOST_INFRASTRUCTURE"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21761320"
    description: "Zenodo archive of GHOST INFRASTRUCTURE v1.1.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21761320"
abstract: >-
  Tests whether present-day 15-minute walking access in Bochum and Essen
  (Ruhr, Germany) is associated with the location of historical coal mines and
  worker colonies beyond what general urban form explains. Accessibility is
  modelled on the full OpenStreetMap pedestrian networks, residents come from the
  Zensus 2022 100 m grid, and settlement age from GHSL built-up surface (1975,
  2020). A naive comparison suggests a legacy effect (Cohen's d = 0.589 in
  Bochum), but a null model of 1,000 random site sets reproduces it (22.5% of
  random sets reach the real effect in Bochum, 83.1% in Essen), and for
  residents the real sites are not distinguishable from random built-up
  locations in either city. Access gaps are instead concentrated among residents
  of areas first built up after 1975 (two to eight times higher rates of no
  access). Version 2.0 replaces the v1.1 "path dependency of centrality" claim
  after an internal review.
keywords:
  - 15-minute city
  - walking accessibility
  - path dependence
  - historical GIS
  - null model
  - Monte Carlo
  - GHSL
  - Zensus 2022
  - Ruhr
  - network analysis
